    "id": "study-forensics-031",
    "title": "Study Forensics: Rapamycin for human longevity — promise vs proof",
    "domain": "study_forensics",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "task_type": "study_forensics",
    "prompt": "Rapamycin is an immunosuppressant that inhibits the mTOR pathway. In mice it reliably extends lifespan by about ten to twenty-five percent, and these results have been replicated across multiple laboratories. Enthusiasts now take the drug off-label to slow aging. However, the first well-designed human trial reported that rapamycin was safe but failed to reduce abdominal fat, its primary goal, and only modestly increased lean muscle in women. The study did not test whether the drug slows biological aging. Experts caution that no clinical evidence yet shows rapamycin slows aging in people, and off-label use carries risks, especially for those with conditions like active cancer.\n",
    "context": "A longevity case juxtaposing strong, replicated preclinical evidence with\nlimited human data. The first human trial was safe but missed its primary\nendpoint. The reporting is cautious but the gap between animal mechanism\nand human evidence remains.\n",
    "expected_answer": "mechanism_vs_evidence",
    "reference_solution": "The article presents rapamycin's strong preclinical track record (reliable 10-25% lifespan extension in mice, replicated) alongside limited human data (safe but failed primary endpoint, modest secondary effects). It honestly notes that no clinical evidence shows rapamycin slows aging in humans. The mechanism is robust in animals but undemonstrated in humans.\n",
